md`# brain-usage

A Cloud Brain recipe: what the Cloudflare account was billed for, by product, read from Cloudflare's billable usage API. It keeps no rows and has one method. Design: \`plan/cloud-brain-cloudflare-usage-read.md\`.

| Method | Cloudflare endpoint |
|---|---|
| \`usage.account\` | \`GET /accounts/ACCOUNT/billable-usage\`, and \`GET /accounts/ACCOUNT/billable-usage/info\` for the anchor day |

\`\`\`
GET /xrpc/com.lopecode.brain.usage.account                                the current billing period
GET /xrpc/com.lopecode.brain.usage.account?from=2026-09-05&to=2026-10-04  a range of days
\`\`\`

\`\`\`js
{ from, to,        // as asked, or null
  anchor,          // billing_cycle_anchor_timestamp of /billable-usage/info, and nothing else of that answer; null when it gave none
  currency,        // BillingCurrency of the first row, or null
  rows: [{ service, family, unit, quantity, pricingUnit, pricingQuantity, cost, cumulatedCost, start, end }],
  total,           // the sum of cost over the rows
  cloudflare: { status, info, ms, cached } }  // the status of each of the two reads, how long both took, and whether this answer was kept
\`\`\`

## The row

A row is one product and one charge period. Of the 27 fields Cloudflare documents for a row ([the API reference](https://developers.cloudflare.com/api/resources/billing/subresources/usage/methods/get_account_usage_v1/), "Version 1, Alpha") ten are kept, renamed (\`usageRow\`), and a field a row lacks is \`null\`:

| Here | Cloudflare's |
|---|---|
| \`service\`, \`family\` | \`ServiceName\`, \`ServiceFamilyName\` |
| \`unit\`, \`quantity\` | \`ConsumedUnit\`, \`ConsumedQuantity\` |
| \`pricingUnit\`, \`pricingQuantity\` | \`PricingUnit\`, \`PricingQuantity\` |
| \`cost\`, \`cumulatedCost\` | \`ContractedCost\`, \`CumulatedContractedCost\` |
| \`start\`, \`end\` | \`ChargePeriodStart\`, \`ChargePeriodEnd\` |

The others are left out: the account's id and name, the invoice's issuer, the subscription's id and the ids and names of the owner's zones are no part of a usage figure. Of \`/billable-usage/info\` only the anchor is answered: the first \`billing_cycle_anchor_timestamp\` of a subscription that has not ended.

## The dates

\`from\` and \`to\` are days, \`YYYY-MM-DD\`, and each is refused (400) unless it is exactly that, as is a \`from\` after its \`to\`; nothing is sent to Cloudflare then. With neither, Cloudflare answers the current billing period. Cloudflare's reference says of a range: "The provided time range must include the subscription billing cycle anchor day, otherwise no usage data is returned."

## Not a pass-through

The Cloudflare token of this Worker also reads the billing profile, the subscriptions and the invoices of the account (the group \`Billing Read\`). So the two paths are constants of the service, the verb is GET, no body is sent, and of what a caller sends only the two dates reach Cloudflare. No other parameter is read.

## Kept 600 s

An answer is kept in the Worker's memory for 600 s under its two dates (\`usageKeep\`), and \`cloudflare.cached\` is \`true\` for an answer from there. Cloudflare's launch post says the data are updated daily. Only a 200 is kept. The memory is one isolate's: another isolate of the Worker asks Cloudflare again.

## Who can call

The owner, or a token of the owner that names the method (\`caller.id == "owner"\`). The figures are the whole Cloudflare account's, which holds more than this Brain. A member, a Worker and a stranger are not answered. A token for it is made once with the owner's session: \`brain.ts token loop-usage usage.account\`, then \`brain.ts curl "/xrpc/com.lopecode.brain.usage.account" --as loop-usage\`.

Calls have no price: Cloudflare states none for the read.

## When it is not answered

| Status | \`error\` | When |
|---|---|---|
| 400 | \`InvalidRequest\` | a date that is not \`YYYY-MM-DD\`, or \`from\` after \`to\` |
| Cloudflare's status | \`CloudflareRefused\` | Cloudflare refused \`/billable-usage\`: \`status\` and \`errors\` are Cloudflare's, each error cut to its \`code\` and \`message\` |
| 502 | \`UpstreamFailure\` | Cloudflare answered 200 with no list of rows; the message names the keys of what it answered, not the values |

A refusal of \`/billable-usage/info\` alone does not fail the call: \`anchor\` is \`null\` and \`cloudflare.info\` is that status.

**When no token is bound** (the deployer's mint failed: its line \`token.mint-failed\`, and it mints again at its next tick) the call is sent with no token and Cloudflare's refusal is the answer, \`CloudflareRefused\`. The service does not tell the two apart: a service that reads \`secrets.CF_TOKEN\` itself declares a secret of that name in its manifest, so only the \`cloudflareApi\` cell reads it. The message of a 400, 401 or 403 names the deployer's line to look for. (The spec has a 502 of the service's own here; as built it is Cloudflare's status.)

## The Cloudflare token

The service declares \`cloudflare: ["Billing Read"]\`. The deployer holds such a deploy for the owner, mints a Cloudflare API token with that one permission group and binds it to this Worker alone. The \`cloudflareApi\` cell sends it, to an address under \`/accounts/ACCOUNT/\` and no other.`
// %%
// A day as a caller gives it. Nothing else of a caller reaches Cloudflare.
usageDay = /^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$/
// %%
// A row as it leaves: ten of Cloudflare's fields, renamed. The account, the invoice, the subscription and the zones are left out.
usageRow = (r) => {
  const of = (name) => (r[name] === undefined ? null : r[name]);
  return { service: of("ServiceName"), family: of("ServiceFamilyName"), unit: of("ConsumedUnit"), quantity: of("ConsumedQuantity"), pricingUnit: of("PricingUnit"), pricingQuantity: of("PricingQuantity"), cost: of("ContractedCost"), cumulatedCost: of("CumulatedContractedCost"), start: of("ChargePeriodStart"), end: of("ChargePeriodEnd") };
}
// %%
// The anchor of /billable-usage/info: of a subscription that has not ended, else of any. Text or null, and nothing else of the answer.
usageAnchor = (info) => {
  const subs = info && Array.isArray(info.subscriptions) ? info.subscriptions.filter((s) => s && typeof s.billing_cycle_anchor_timestamp === "string") : [];
  const one = subs.find((s) => !s.end_timestamp) || subs[0];
  return one ? one.billing_cycle_anchor_timestamp : null;
}
// %%
// Answers kept in memory for `ms` under a key. `now` is given by the caller, so a test moves the time.
usageKeep = (ms = 600000, most = 100) => {
  const kept = new Map();
  return {
    get: (key, now) => {
      const k = kept.get(key);
      return k && now - k.at < ms ? k.value : null;
    },
    set: (key, now, value) => {
      if (kept.size >= most && !kept.has(key)) kept.clear();
      kept.set(key, { at: now, value });
    }
  };
}
// %%
usage_service = {
  const kept = usageKeep();
  const json = (status, body) => new Response(JSON.stringify(body), { status, headers: { "content-type": "application/json" } });
  return cloudflare.Service(
    "usage",
    async (request) => {
      const url = new URL(request.url);
      if (url.pathname.split(".").pop() !== "account") return json(404, { error: "MethodNotImplemented", message: "usage.account" });
      const day = (name) => (url.searchParams.has(name) ? url.searchParams.get(name) : null);
      const from = day("from"), to = day("to");
      if ([from, to].some((d) => d !== null && !usageDay.test(d)) || (from !== null && to !== null && from > to)) return json(400, { error: "InvalidRequest", message: "from and to are days, YYYY-MM-DD, from not after to" });
      const key = JSON.stringify([from, to]), had = kept.get(key, Date.now());
      if (had) return json(200, { ...had, cloudflare: { ...had.cloudflare, cached: true } });
      const range = [["from", from], ["to", to]].filter(([, d]) => d !== null).map(([name, d]) => name + "=" + d).join("&");
      const started = Date.now();
      // The two paths are constants; the query is the two days, which are digits and hyphens.
      const [usage, info] = await Promise.all([cloudflareApi.fetch("/billable-usage" + (range ? "?" + range : ""), { method: "GET" }), cloudflareApi.fetch("/billable-usage/info", { method: "GET" })]);
      const data = await usage.json().catch(() => null), about = await info.json().catch(() => null);
      if (!usage.ok) {
        const errors = (data && Array.isArray(data.errors) ? data.errors : []).slice(0, 10).map((e) => ({ code: e && e.code, message: String((e && e.message) || "").slice(0, 300) }));
        // The service cannot read whether a token is bound (naming secrets.CF_TOKEN would declare a secret), so it says where to look.
        const unbound = [400, 401, 403].includes(usage.status) ? ". When the deployer's lines have token.mint-failed for brain-x-usage, no token is bound to this Worker and Cloudflare refuses the call it is sent without one" : "";
        return json(usage.status, { error: "CloudflareRefused", message: `Cloudflare answered ${usage.status} to /billable-usage${unbound}`, status: usage.status, errors });
      }
      if (!data || !Array.isArray(data.result)) return json(502, { error: "UpstreamFailure", message: `Cloudflare answered ${usage.status} with no list of rows: ${data && typeof data === "object" ? "keys " + Object.keys(data).slice(0, 10).join(", ") + "; result is " + (data.result === null ? "null" : typeof data.result) : "no JSON"}` });
      const listed = data.result.filter((r) => r && typeof r === "object"), rows = listed.map(usageRow);
      const answer = {
        from,
        to,
        anchor: info.ok ? usageAnchor(about && about.result) : null,
        currency: listed.length && typeof listed[0].BillingCurrency === "string" ? listed[0].BillingCurrency : null,
        rows,
        total: rows.reduce((sum, r) => sum + (typeof r.cost === "number" ? r.cost : 0), 0),
        cloudflare: { status: usage.status, info: info.status, ms: Date.now() - started, cached: false }
      };
      kept.set(key, Date.now(), answer);
      return json(200, answer);
    },
    {
      cloudflare: ["Billing Read"],
      methods: {
        // The owner, or a token of the owner that names it. The figures are the whole account's: no member is answered.
        "com.lopecode.brain.usage.account": { type: "query", allow: 'caller.id == "owner"' }
      }
    }
  );
}
// %%
usage_announce = {
  plugins.add("workers", usage_service, { invalidation });
  return "announced usage_service";
}
// %%
md`## Tests`
// %%
// A row with each of the 27 fields of Cloudflare's reference. The values are the reference's example, with made-up names in the fields that are left out.
usageRow27 = (more = {}) => ({
  BilledCost: 0.75, BillingAccountId: "acct-id-0123", BillingAccountName: "Example Account", BillingCurrency: "USD", BillingPeriodStart: "2025-02-01T00:00:00Z",
  ChargeCategory: "Usage", ChargeClass: null, ChargeDescription: "Workers Standard requests", ChargePeriodEnd: "2025-02-02T00:00:00Z", ChargePeriodStart: "2025-02-01T00:00:00Z",
  ConsumedQuantity: 150000, ConsumedUnit: "Requests", ContractedCost: 0.75, CumulatedContractedCost: 2.25, CumulatedPricingQuantity: 4500000, EffectiveCost: 0.75,
  HostProviderName: "Cloudflare", InvoiceIssuerName: "Issuer Inc", ListCost: 0.75, PricingQuantity: 150000, PricingUnit: "Count", ServiceName: "Workers Standard",
  ServiceProviderName: "Cloudflare", ServiceFamilyName: "Workers", SubscriptionId: "sub-0123", ZoneId: "zone-0123", ZoneName: "owner-zone.example",
  ...more
})
// %%
// Cloudflare as far as usage.account reads it. `seen` has each request it was sent. `usage` and `info` are a function of the request, or a value.
usageRig = async ({ usage, info, secrets = { CF_TOKEN: "minted-1", BRAIN_KEY: "k" } } = {}) => {
  const seen = [];
  const cf = (result, status = 200) => new Response(JSON.stringify(status < 400 ? { success: true, errors: [], messages: [], result } : result), { status, headers: { "content-type": "application/json" } });
  const sim = await simulate(usage_service, {
    secrets,
    config: { base: "cb4", account: "acct" },
    fetch: async (request) => {
      const url = new URL(request.url);
      seen.push({ method: request.method, url: request.url, path: url.pathname, search: url.search, authorization: request.headers.get("authorization"), body: await request.text() });
      const which = url.pathname.endsWith("/info") ? info : usage;
      // Cloudflare refuses a call that does not carry the minted token.
      if (request.headers.get("authorization") !== "Bearer minted-1") return cf({ success: false, errors: [{ code: 10000, message: "Authentication error" }] }, 401);
      if (typeof which === "function") return which(request, cf);
      return url.pathname.endsWith("/info") ? cf(which === undefined ? { covered: true, subscriptions: [{ id: "sub-0123", billing_cycle_anchor_timestamp: "2025-01-05T00:00:00Z", start_timestamp: "2024-01-05T00:00:00Z" }] } : which) : cf(which === undefined ? [usageRow27()] : which);
    }
  });
  const get = async (query = "", init = {}) => {
    const r = await sim.fetch("https://usage.internal/xrpc/com.lopecode.brain.usage." + query, { headers: { "x-brain-caller": "owner", "x-brain-via": "delegation:b6559fa96c36", "x-brain-holder": "secret:loop-usage", "x-brain-tab": "0" }, ...init });
    return { status: r.status, data: await r.json() };
  };
  return { sim, seen, get, cf };
}
// %%
// R2 and R3 of plan/cloud-brain-cloudflare-usage-read.md: whatever is asked, what reaches Cloudflare is a GET of one of two paths, with the two days and nothing else.
test_usage_sends_two_fixed_gets_and_of_a_caller_only_two_days = {
  const rig = await usageRig();
  const A = "https://api.cloudflare.com/client/v4/accounts/acct/billable-usage", I = A + "/info";
  // No dates: the current billing period, and the anchor.
  expect((await rig.get("account")).status).toBe(200);
  expect(rig.seen.map((s) => s.url).sort()).toEqual([A, I]);
  // Two dates, with what else a caller may add: only the dates are sent on.
  const out = await rig.get("account?from=2026-09-05&to=2026-10-04&path=/tokens&worker=brain-core&from=2020-01-01");
  expect([out.status, out.data.from, out.data.to]).toEqual([200, "2026-09-05", "2026-10-04"]);
  expect(rig.seen.slice(2).map((s) => s.url).sort()).toEqual([I, A + "?from=2026-09-05&to=2026-10-04"]);
  // One date alone is a range Cloudflare closes.
  expect((await rig.get("account?to=2026-10-04")).status).toBe(200);
  expect(rig.seen.slice(4).map((s) => s.url).sort()).toEqual([A + "/info", A + "?to=2026-10-04"]);
  // A POST with a body is answered as the GET: the body is not read and none is sent.
  expect((await rig.get("account?from=2026-01-01", { method: "POST", headers: { "x-brain-caller": "owner", "content-type": "application/json" }, body: '{"path":"/tokens"}' })).status).toBe(200);
  const asked = rig.seen.length;
  expect(asked).toBe(8);
  // Anything that is not a day is refused, and nothing is sent.
  const bad = ["account?from=../tokens", "account?from=", "account?to=", "account?from=2026-9-5", "account?from=2026-09-05T00:00:00Z", "account?from=2026-09-05%26x=1", "account?from=2026-09-05/../tokens", "account?from=20260905", "account?from=2026-10-04&to=2026-09-05", "account?from=2026-09-05&to=tomorrow", "account?from=%202026-09-05"];
  for (const q of bad) {
    const r = await rig.get(q);
    expect([q, r.status, r.data.error]).toEqual([q, 400, "InvalidRequest"]);
  }
  // A method the service does not have is not sent on either.
  for (const other of ["info", "account/../../tokens", "query", "account.info"]) expect([other, (await rig.get(other)).status]).toEqual([other, 404]);
  expect(rig.seen.length).toBe(asked);
  // Every request Cloudflare was sent: a GET, one of the two paths, the minted token, no body, a query of days.
  for (const s of rig.seen) {
    expect(s.method).toBe("GET");
    expect(["/client/v4/accounts/acct/billable-usage", "/client/v4/accounts/acct/billable-usage/info"]).toContain(s.path);
    expect(s.search).toMatch(/^(\?(from|to)=\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}(&to=\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2})?)?$/);
    expect([s.authorization, s.body]).toEqual(["Bearer minted-1", ""]);
  }
  return asked + " requests, " + bad.length + " refused with none";
}
// %%
// R3: the manifest, and the rule against five callers.
test_usage_declares_billing_read_and_answers_the_owner_and_no_other = {
  const { meta, parts } = await usage_service.emit();
  expect(meta.worker).toBe("brain-x-usage");
  expect(meta.cloudflare).toEqual(["Billing Read"]);
  expect([meta.resources, meta.secrets, meta.calls]).toEqual([[], [], []]);
  expect(Object.entries(meta.access).map(([nsid, a]) => [nsid, a.type, a.allow, a.price])).toEqual([["com.lopecode.brain.usage.account", "query", 'caller.id == "owner"', undefined]]);
  // The token is read from the binding the deployer made, and from nowhere a caller can set.
  expect(parts[0].text).toContain("__rt.platform.secrets.CF_TOKEN");
  const may = (who) => decide({ allow: meta.access["com.lopecode.brain.usage.account"].allow }, { caller: callerOf(who), origin: callerOf(who), request: { params: {} } }).allow;
  const callers = [{ caller: "owner", via: "session", tab: true }, { caller: "owner", via: "delegation:b6559fa96c36", holder: "secret:loop-usage", tab: false }, { caller: "did:plc:member" }, { caller: "worker:brain-x-echo" }, { caller: "anonymous" }];
  expect(callers.map(may)).toEqual([true, true, false, false, false]);
  return meta.cloudflare[0];
}
// %%
// R3: of Cloudflare's 27 fields a row keeps ten, and of the info answer only the anchor leaves.
test_usage_a_row_has_ten_names_and_nothing_of_the_account_leaves = {
  const names = ["service", "family", "unit", "quantity", "pricingUnit", "pricingQuantity", "cost", "cumulatedCost", "start", "end"];
  expect(Object.keys(usageRow27()).length).toBe(27);
  const second = usageRow27({ ServiceName: "R2 Storage", ServiceFamilyName: undefined, ConsumedUnit: "GB-months", ConsumedQuantity: 3, PricingQuantity: 3, ContractedCost: 0.5, CumulatedContractedCost: 0.5, BillingCurrency: "EUR" });
  delete second.ServiceFamilyName;
  const rig = await usageRig({ usage: [usageRow27(), second, null, "a row that is text"], info: { covered: true, subscriptions: [{ id: "sub-ended", billing_cycle_anchor_timestamp: "2023-03-03T00:00:00Z", start_timestamp: "2023-03-03T00:00:00Z", end_timestamp: "2024-01-01T00:00:00Z" }, { id: "sub-0123", billing_cycle_anchor_timestamp: "2025-01-05T00:00:00Z", start_timestamp: "2024-01-05T00:00:00Z" }] } });
  const out = await rig.get("account");
  expect(out.status).toBe(200);
  expect(Object.keys(out.data)).toEqual(["from", "to", "anchor", "currency", "rows", "total", "cloudflare"]);
  expect(out.data.rows.map((r) => Object.keys(r))).toEqual([names, names]);
  expect(out.data.rows[0]).toEqual({ service: "Workers Standard", family: "Workers", unit: "Requests", quantity: 150000, pricingUnit: "Count", pricingQuantity: 150000, cost: 0.75, cumulatedCost: 2.25, start: "2025-02-01T00:00:00Z", end: "2025-02-02T00:00:00Z" });
  // A field a row lacks is null, not left out.
  expect([out.data.rows[1].service, out.data.rows[1].family, out.data.rows[1].cost]).toEqual(["R2 Storage", null, 0.5]);
  // The anchor of the subscription that has not ended, the first row's currency, the sum of cost.
  expect([out.data.from, out.data.to, out.data.anchor, out.data.currency, out.data.total]).toEqual([null, null, "2025-01-05T00:00:00Z", "USD", 1.25]);
  expect(Object.keys(out.data.cloudflare)).toEqual(["status", "info", "ms", "cached"]);
  expect([out.data.cloudflare.status, out.data.cloudflare.info, out.data.cloudflare.cached]).toEqual([200, 200, false]);
  // Nothing of the 17 other fields, or of the info answer but the anchor.
  expect(JSON.stringify(out.data)).not.toMatch(/Example Account|acct-id|Issuer|sub-|zone|covered|subscriptions|2024-01-05|2023-03-03|minted/i);
  // usageAnchor on what is not the documented shape.
  expect([usageAnchor(null), usageAnchor({}), usageAnchor({ subscriptions: "x" }), usageAnchor({ subscriptions: [null, { billing_cycle_anchor_timestamp: 7 }] }), usageAnchor({ subscriptions: [{ billing_cycle_anchor_timestamp: "a", end_timestamp: "b" }] })]).toEqual([null, null, null, null, "a"]);
  // No rows: an empty list, no currency, a total of 0.
  const none = await (await usageRig({ usage: [] })).get("account");
  expect([none.status, none.data.rows, none.data.currency, none.data.total]).toEqual([200, [], null, 0]);
  return names.length + " names of 27";
}
// %%
// An answer is kept 600 s under its two dates, and only a 200.
test_usage_keeps_an_answer_600_s_under_its_two_dates = {
  // The time is the caller's, so the 600 s are shown without a clock.
  const k = usageKeep();
  k.set("a", 1000, "A");
  expect([k.get("a", 1000), k.get("a", 600999), k.get("a", 601000), k.get("b", 1000)]).toEqual(["A", "A", null, null]);
  const small = usageKeep(10, 2);
  small.set("a", 0, 1); small.set("b", 0, 2); small.set("b", 1, 3);
  expect([small.get("a", 1), small.get("b", 1)]).toEqual([1, 3]);
  small.set("c", 1, 4);
  expect([small.get("a", 1), small.get("b", 1), small.get("c", 1)]).toEqual([null, null, 4]);
  // Through the method: the second call of the same dates asks Cloudflare nothing and says so.
  let n = 0;
  const rig = await usageRig({ usage: (request, cf) => cf([usageRow27({ ContractedCost: ++n })]) });
  const first = await rig.get("account?from=2026-09-05&to=2026-10-04");
  const again = await rig.get("account?from=2026-09-05&to=2026-10-04");
  expect([first.data.cloudflare.cached, again.data.cloudflare.cached, rig.seen.length]).toEqual([false, true, 2]);
  expect({ ...again.data, cloudflare: null }).toEqual({ ...first.data, cloudflare: null });
  // Other dates, and no dates, are other answers.
  const other = await rig.get("account?from=2026-09-06&to=2026-10-04");
  const period = await rig.get("account");
  expect([other.data.cloudflare.cached, other.data.total, period.data.cloudflare.cached, period.data.total, rig.seen.length]).toEqual([false, 2, false, 3, 6]);
  expect((await rig.get("account")).data.cloudflare.cached).toBe(true);
  // A refusal is not kept: the next call asks again.
  let refuse = true;
  const down = await usageRig({ usage: (request, cf) => (refuse ? cf({ success: false, errors: [{ code: 10000, message: "Authentication error" }] }, 403) : cf([usageRow27()])) });
  expect((await down.get("account")).status).toBe(403);
  refuse = false;
  const up = await down.get("account");
  expect([up.status, up.data.cloudflare.cached, down.seen.length]).toEqual([200, false, 4]);
  return "kept, and asked again for other dates and after a refusal";
}
// %%
// Cloudflare's refusal leaves with its status and its errors, and with no token bound the refusal names the deployer's line.
test_usage_passes_on_cloudflares_refusal_and_names_the_mint_when_it_may_be_the_cause = {
  // No CF_TOKEN binding, as after token.mint-failed: the call goes with no token, and the refusal is Cloudflare's.
  const bare = await usageRig({ secrets: { BRAIN_KEY: "k" } });
  const no = await bare.get("account");
  expect([no.status, no.data.error, no.data.status, no.data.errors]).toEqual([401, "CloudflareRefused", 401, [{ code: 10000, message: "Authentication error" }]]);
  expect(no.data.message).toContain("token.mint-failed for brain-x-usage");
  expect(bare.seen.map((s) => s.authorization)).toEqual(["Bearer undefined", "Bearer undefined"]);
  // Cloudflare refuses the token: its status, and of each error the code and the message.
  const refused = await usageRig({ usage: (request, cf) => cf({ success: false, errors: [{ code: 10000, message: "Authentication error", documentation_url: "https://example.invalid/x", BillingAccountName: "Example Account" }], result: { BillingAccountName: "Example Account" } }, 403) });
  const out = await refused.get("account");
  expect(out.data.message).toMatch(/^Cloudflare answered 403 to \/billable-usage\. When the deployer's lines have token\.mint-failed/);
  expect({ ...out, data: { ...out.data, message: null } }).toEqual({ status: 403, data: { error: "CloudflareRefused", message: null, status: 403, errors: [{ code: 10000, message: "Authentication error" }] } });
  expect(JSON.stringify(out.data)).not.toMatch(/Example Account|documentation_url/);
  // A refusal that is not about the token says nothing of the mint.
  const limited = await (await usageRig({ usage: (request, cf) => cf({ success: false, errors: [{ code: 971, message: "rate limited" }] }, 429) })).get("account");
  expect(limited.data).toEqual({ error: "CloudflareRefused", message: "Cloudflare answered 429 to /billable-usage", status: 429, errors: [{ code: 971, message: "rate limited" }] });
  // A refusal that is not JSON has no errors to pass on.
  const html = await (await usageRig({ usage: () => new Response("<html>bad gateway</html>", { status: 502 }) })).get("account");
  expect([html.status, html.data.error, html.data.errors]).toEqual([502, "CloudflareRefused", []]);
  // 200 with no list: the keys of the answer are named, not its values.
  const odd = await (await usageRig({ usage: () => new Response(JSON.stringify({ success: true, result: { BillingAccountName: "Example Account" } }), { headers: { "content-type": "application/json" } }) })).get("account");
  expect([odd.status, odd.data.error]).toEqual([502, "UpstreamFailure"]);
  expect(odd.data.message).toBe("Cloudflare answered 200 with no list of rows: keys success, result; result is object");
  // The info read alone refused: the rows are answered, with no anchor and that status.
  const half = await (await usageRig({ info: (request, cf) => cf({ success: false, errors: [{ code: 7003, message: "No route" }] }, 404) })).get("account");
  expect([half.status, half.data.anchor, half.data.cloudflare.info, half.data.rows.length]).toEqual([200, null, 404, 1]);
  return "401, 403, 429 and 502 passed on";
}
// %%
import {cloudflare, cloudflareApi, simulate, decide, callerOf} from "@tomlarkworthy/cloudflare-iac"
// %%
import {plugins} from "@tomlarkworthy/plugin-registry"
// %%
import {expect} from "@tomlarkworthy/jest-expect-standalone"
